| type | Media type of the file | {{params}} |
| description | Description of the file | {{params}} |
| key | Encryption algorithm, key and nonce | {{params}} |
| expires | Intended availability of the file | {{params}} |

# Security Considerations {#security}

**Access.**  Anyone with the message can fetch the file, as with an
attachment.  But URLs leak more easily, through browser history, logs
or Referer header fields, so encryption keeps a leaked "url" alone from
revealing the file.  The download link includes the key, so it is as
sensitive as the message, and the attachment server sees the key when
it is used.  An attachment server could instead put the key in the
fragment and decrypt in the browser.

**Guessable URLs.**  A guessable URL lets anyone fetch the file, or
learn that it exists and its size.

**Changed content.**  The digest detects a changed file.  It is only as
trustworthy as the header field; a DKIM signature binds it to the
signing domain.  Anyone with the key can create a valid ciphertext, so
the authentication of the AEAD algorithm alone doesn't detect a
replaced file.

**Request forgery.**  Receiving servers MUST NOT fetch from addresses
that aren't publicly routable, such as loopback, link-local or private
addresses, and MUST check again after each redirect.

**Resource use.**  Receiving servers SHOULD limit the number and total
size of linked attachments they fetch per message.

**Denial of service.**  Someone who receives a message with linked
attachments can resend it, or just its Content-Link-Attachment header
fields,
to millions of recipients, each of whose servers then fetches the file
from the original attachment server.  The same works with a "url" that
points at any large file on any server.  Forwarding MUAs that publish
their own copies ({{forwarding}}) avoid this for ordinary forwarding.
Receiving servers SHOULD fetch a URL only once, and reuse the result
for every message that names the same URL and digest, and SHOULD limit
how fast they fetch from any one host.  Attachment servers MAY limit how
often they serve a file, and receivers MUST back off when they do
({{fetching}}).

**Misleading links.**  The body links might not match the header
fields.  An MUA that hides them and shows the verified file avoids
this.

**Scanning.**  The key is in the message, so systems that handle the
message can decrypt the file.  Encryption protects it only from the
attachment server and from anyone with the URL alone.

**Tracking.**  If an MUA fetches a linked attachment from the
attachment server when the user views the message or opens the file,
the sender learns when, and from where, it happened.  An image shown
through a "cid" URL ({{cid-urls}}) is then a "web bug"
({{Section 9.9 of E2E-GUIDANCE}}).  So a message store SHOULD, within
the limits of {{fetching}}, fetch all linked attachments when the
message arrives, and store them with the message.  MUAs SHOULD get them
from the message store, so that nothing is fetched from the attachment
server when the user views the message or opens an attachment.  Remote
images have the same problem, and a message store can proxy them in the
same way.

# Privacy Considerations {#privacy}

The attachment server sees each fetch.  If an MUA fetches the file when
the message is displayed, the sender learns when and where it was read,
as with a remote image, and per-recipient URLs show who read it.
Fetching by the receiving server on arrival shows only that the message
was delivered, and hides the recipient's address.

